# This repo keeps NO secrets inside the working tree.
# The real values live in three files outside the repo, all chmod 600:
#
#   ~/secrets/clickhouse.env   ClickHouse credentials. Used by docker compose
#                              (env_file) and by the tests.
#   ~/secrets/api-keys.env     API keys. Optional: only needed by code that
#                              talks to Alchemy or Nansen (backfill, checks).
#   ~/secrets/agent-reader.env ClickHouse credentials of the read-only user
#                              (clickhouse/users.d/agent_reader.xml). Optional:
#                              only needed by code that reads the marts as
#                              that user.
#
# They are split so that an unattended process can be given database access
# without being able to read the API keys (see DECISIONS.md), and so that a
# reader can be given the read-only user without the one that can write.
#
# If your files live somewhere else, copy this file to .env (git-ignored) and
# set the pointers below. .env holds PATHS ONLY, never secret values.
# Both docker compose and src/univ3_indexer/config.py read these pointers.

CLICKHOUSE_ENV_FILE=
API_KEYS_ENV_FILE=
AGENT_READER_ENV_FILE=

# --- contents expected in clickhouse.env -----------------------------------
# CLICKHOUSE_DB=
# CLICKHOUSE_USER=
# CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD=
# CLICKHOUSE_DEFAULT_ACCESS_MANAGEMENT=
# CH_HOST=
# CH_PORT=
# AGENT_READER_PASSWORD_SHA256=   SHA-256 (hex) of the read-only user's password.
#                                 The server reads it at start-up and does NOT start
#                                 without it: set it before `make up`
#                                 (docs/AGENT_ACCESS.md). The password itself goes in
#                                 agent-reader.env, never here.

# --- contents expected in api-keys.env -------------------------------------
# ALCHEMY_API_KEY=
# NANSEN_API_KEY=

# --- contents expected in agent-reader.env ---------------------------------
# Its own names, host and port included: a variable in the environment wins
# over the file, so shared names would let an exported clickhouse.env turn the
# reader into the user that can write.
# AGENT_READER_HOST=
# AGENT_READER_PORT=
# AGENT_READER_DB=
# AGENT_READER_USER=
# AGENT_READER_PASSWORD=
